    <rigor-hint>FULL</rigor-hint>
    <rigor-reason>R1's highest-risk, SECURITY-SENSITIVE area (root §6, NFR-06): BFF query-filter enforcement of message/thread privacy, internal-only, and privilege classification. A defect leaks privileged/private content across the record-security boundary. Touches `.cs`; explicit `code-review` + `adr-check` at Step 9.5 are UNCONDITIONALLY REQUIRED.</rigor-reason>
    <parallel-group>W4</parallel-group>
    <parallel-safe>true</parallel-safe>
    <fr-refs>FR-08, NFR-06</fr-refs>
    <model-tier>opus</model-tier>
    <effort>xhigh</effort>
  </metadata>

  <prompt>
    Add BFF query-filter enforcement so a caller only ever sees messages/threads they are authorized to read — composed from EXISTING Dataverse record security, not a new authorization engine (design §5, owner directive: leverage Spaarke UAC / Dataverse security; do not rebuild core access control).

    Three composable dimensions on `sprk_communication` / `sprk_communicationthread` (columns added in task 005):
    • **Privacy** — message-level AND thread-level. A private thread's messages are invisible to users without an explicit per-record sharing grant (mechanism from task 002). Thread-level privacy lives on the thread entity.
    • **Internal-only** — a user attribute (D-05). Internal-only messages are invisible to non-internal users (external participants in R2/R3 never see them).
    • **Privilege** — classification metadata, DISTINCT from privacy; composes with it. **AI may FLAG but NEVER DECIDE** privilege (ADR-015) — enforcement acts on the classification field, never on an AI verdict.

    **Point-forward privacy switch**: flipping a thread to open exposes ONLY messages from that moment forward; prior private messages STAY restricted (retroactive open is a separate audited action, out of R1).

    **Invariants (NFR-06, security-sensitive):** no privileged/private content leaks; ACS membership never exceeds Dataverse-derived access (this task's read filter and task 041's membership projection MUST agree). This is a BFF read-path filter — the access boundary is the Dataverse record. Explicit `code-review` + `adr-check` at Step 9.5 are REQUIRED.
  </prompt>

  <role>
    Spaarke BFF security engineer for the Communication subsystem. Authority on BFF authorization seams (ADR-003), endpoint filters (ADR-008), Dataverse record security composition, ADR-015 (AI flags never decides), ADR-024 (regarding anchor), §11 Component Justification, `.claude/constraints/bff-extensions.md`, and the design §5 privacy model. You treat every read filter as a leak-prevention control and default to deny.
  </role>

  <goal>
    BFF read paths for `sprk_communication` / `sprk_communicationthread` enforce, in composition: message-level + thread-level privacy (invisible without an explicit grant), internal-only (invisible to non-internal users via the D-05 attribute), and privilege classification (composes with privacy; acts on the field, never an AI verdict — ADR-015). The privacy switch is point-forward (prior private messages stay restricted). No privileged/private content leaks; the read filter agrees with task 041's ACS membership projection (ACS ⊆ Dataverse-derived access). Build clean; publish-size + CVE + bff-extensions Placement Justification reported; explicit `code-review` + `adr-check` at Step 9.5 (security-sensitive); `/conflict-check` clean before PR.
  </goal>

  <context>
    <background>
      Design §5: "The access boundary is the Dataverse record, exactly as with email. The `sprk_communication` (and `sprk_communicationthread`) record wraps the message and IS the authoritative access boundary — whoever can read the record can read the message." This task adds a small number of fields (task 005) + BFF query filters + point-forward logic on top of existing record security. NO new authorization engine.

      **Component Justification (§11) for the enforcement filter:**
      1. **Existing** — BFF authorization seams (ADR-003) + endpoint filters (ADR-008) + Dataverse record security already exist; task 005 added the privacy/internal-only/privilege columns. This task adds the composition/filter logic, not a new engine (verify by grep).
      2. **Extension** — Extend the existing BFF read path with a privacy-composition filter; reuse Dataverse record security for the base boundary and the task-002 grant primitive for private threads.
      3. **Cost-of-doing-nothing (name the concrete leak that fails without this)** — Without enforcement, the thread-read endpoint (task 050) returns EVERY `sprk_communication` row on a thread to any caller who can read the thread record — so a **privileged attorney-work-product message, or a private-thread message the caller was never granted, or an internal-only message, is returned to an unauthorized user in the timeline poll response**. That is a concrete confidentiality breach (NFR-06), not "future flexibility."

      **AI boundary (ADR-015):** privilege is a classification FIELD. AI may set/suggest a flag for human review; enforcement reads the field. Enforcement MUST NOT call AI or act on an AI verdict at read time — flag-never-decide.

      **Consistency with task 041:** this read filter and the membership-reconcile projection are two views of the same rule (Dataverse-derived access). They MUST agree — the read filter is the authoritative in-request check; ACS membership is the reconciled transport projection. Neither may exceed Dataverse-derived access.

      **Scope discipline:** filter + point-forward logic + fields wiring. Do NOT build a new ACL, a new sharing engine, or retroactive bulk-open tooling (out of R1). Do NOT let AI decide privilege.
    </background>
