  <acceptance-criteria>
    <criterion testable="true">A private thread's messages are INVISIBLE to a user without an explicit grant (negative/authorization case).</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">An internal-only message is INVISIBLE to a non-internal user (D-05 attribute) (negative/authorization case).</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">A privileged message is NOT returned to an unauthorized caller; privilege composes with privacy; enforcement acts on the field, not an AI verdict (ADR-015 — no AI call at read time).</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Point-forward switch: flipping a thread to open exposes ONLY messages authored after the open timestamp; prior private messages STAY restricted (negative case).</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">The BFF read filter set is a subset of Dataverse-derived access and agrees with task 041's ACS-membership projection (no read exceeds membership) — verified by test.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Enforcement composes from existing Dataverse record security (no new authorization engine); default-deny — a row is visible only if it passes every dimension.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">`dotnet build` clean; publish-size delta reported vs ~45.30 MB; no new HIGH CVE; explicit `code-review` + `adr-check` at Step 9.5 completed with all Critical findings resolved; `/conflict-check` clean.</criterion>
  </acceptance-criteria>

  <notes>
    R1's highest-risk area. Depends on 040 (thread identity), 005 (privacy columns), 002 (private-grant primitive). Blocks 050 (the thread-read endpoint MUST apply this filter). Parallel-safe with 041/043 after 040.

    Default-deny is the design stance: if it is unclear whether a caller may see a row, hide it and escalate (root §6). Do NOT optimize the filter into an allow-by-default shape for performance — correctness dominates (NFR-06). Do NOT let AI decide privilege (ADR-015) — enforcement reads a human-owned field. If strict composition seems to force a worse outcome, surface it via §6.5 rather than silently deviating.

    Retroactive bulk-open tooling is explicitly OUT of R1 — build only the point-forward switch.
  </notes>

